{"trans": "Advocate fair competition, oppose deceit and trickery, and promote a flourishing and diverse internet; advocate unity and collaboration, oppose harming others for personal gain, and promote mutual benefit and win-win results online."}
{"trans": "In short, more overseas companies may find that the costs of listing in the US outweigh the benefits and thus choose to leave, but this in no way means they are prepared to abandon the American market."}
{"trans": "He believes that in such a panicky market environment, it is next to impossible for gold to remain unscathed, and that any talk of breaking the $1000 mark is likely just a pipe dream."}
{"trans": "The police officer explained that the students' actions were an act of heroism. They stepped forward bravely at the critical moment when the elderly person fell into the water, which is highly commendable. It is understood that the elderly person suffers from a mild mental illness. After receiving the hospital's full emergency treatment, they are now out of danger."}
{"trans": "Of course, the methods above are just a few of the limited yet effective strategies for review. The arrangements for the final review sprint should be diverse and are by no means exhaustive; they should also be chosen based on the individual characteristics of each candidate. Wishing all candidates triumphant and swift success"}
{"trans": "Flowing into another long river of this life, this world.\nThe place where Zhaojun dismounted,\nthe stones of history are still speaking.\nThe grass is the green of those years,\nthe flowers have the fragrance of the past.\nA herd of cattle and sheep do not recognize today,\nwith heads bowed, humble and cautious.\nIn meticulous time, drinking water and remembering its source.\nPeople sit in the aroma of wine.\nAutumn waters of South Lake, a smokeless night."}
{"trans": "This conviction is not about seeking a life of mere material comfort, nor of pursuing great longevity. One cannot live twice, so how can one realize the meaning and value of life before death? Even if it means being shattered to pieces, one must leave a resonant echo, like jade striking the ground with a clear, ringing sound, and burst forth with the brilliance of life into the world."}
{"trans": "The others managed to remain calm, raising their guns to survey the surroundings for any sign of the enemy. Only Li Wenbin was in a frantic mess. He dove headfirst into a clump of grass, his rear end sticking high in the air and his hands covering his head in a pathetic display. His companions covered their faces and snickered, and Li Wenbin's prestige instantly plummeted."}
{"trans": "\"How do you plan to be king?\" the animals asked, half-doubtingly. \"First, I will mediate disputes, remaining impartial, fair, and reasonable. Second, I will handle the affairs of state diligently and conscientiously, without complaint. Third, when facing a powerful enemy, I will lead the charge and be the first to rush into battle. This is my inaugural address.\""}
{"trans": "He was worldly-wise, long familiar with the vicissitudes of life, and could turn like a compass needle. His North Star was ever-changing and unfixed. Thanks to his ingratiating sweet talk, he was so despicable as to never have met with disaster; furthermore, he was torrentially and ceaselessly eloquent (unless he wasn't paid enough)."}
{"trans": "When I first followed my colleagues to the stock exchange, I was destined to form an unbreakable bond with stocks. Seventeen years of springs, summers, autumns, and winters have let me taste all the joys and sorrows of the stock market. After several ups and downs, I have become an old veteran, and the past remains vivid in my mind! 1. The sea of bitterness has no bounds, repent and the shore is at hand."}
{"trans": "The routed soldiers and generals fled for a long time before finally stopping. He looked up to the sky and sighed, \"I raised an army at the age of twenty-five, was invincible in every battle and victorious in every siege. Now, in my twilight years, to have suffered such a crushing defeat—it truly infuriates me!\" When the news of the victory in the Battle of Ningyuan reached the imperial court, the entire court was wild with joy. Everyone raised their cups to celebrate"}
{"trans": "For newlyweds, friends and family visit in a constant stream to offer their blessings. The living room decor should not be too unconventional; the emphasis should be on comfort and a welcoming feel. It should be comfortable for you to live in, while also making guests feel at home. Soft fabric sofas and throw pillows are essential elements."}
{"trans": "I said, \"Stop over-generalizing. Let's just stick to the matter at hand.\" My son replied, \"For years, I've been a detached observer of your attitude, and I feel you treat the computer as something too sacred. You're afraid to touch this, and you can't move that. The biggest mental block for you moms when it comes to learning is that you worship computers like gods.\""}
{"trans": "Always on edge, afraid of saying the wrong thing, afraid of doing the wrong thing, afraid of offending people. Seventh, avoid sorrow: Throughout life, some unfortunate events often come to mind, and one is overcome with sorrow without realizing it. Eighth, avoid suspicion: A suspicious person always thinks others are plotting against them, and must be on guard against every word and action, thus feeling restless and often suffering from insomnia."}
{"trans": "In a shameful corner there was also a pathetic guillotine, furtive, alarmed and shamefaced, as if it were constantly on tenterhooks, terrified of being caught in the act of its evil deeds—for each time it had finished its dirty business, it would immediately make a quick getaway. How could one not feel comforted by all of this"}
{"trans": "This is when the true charm of a fight lies in the restless anticipation after it is arranged, the tense standoff before it begins, the war of words when it is being broken up, the clinking of glasses after it disperses, the supreme sense of brotherhood when meeting again, and fighting shoulder-to-shoulder in the next brawl. As this cycle repeats, the ranks of pacifists grow ever larger."}
{"trans": "The Baron's unbridled insults are simply intolerable; just you wait, you shameless scoundrel, for honor's sake, I will take on the duty of vengeance. With such flagrant insults from the crowd, only he can bear the responsibility for revenge; he cannot suffer this in silence, even at the cost of his life."}
{"trans": "It looked like there would be no argument, no tempest, as things were now calm and peaceful. But Mom once again asked at an inopportune moment, \"Would you like to see what I looked like when I was your age?\" The daughter's reply was also strange, and equally inopportune: \"Later, dear Mom, let's look later.\""}
{"trans": "In terms of conducting himself in the world, he was still in the early stages, but when it came to scholarship, he was completely confident. The Director, however eloquent, could hardly be more tricky and peculiar than the professors at a master's thesis defense. Ask a woodcutter how to eat Western food, and he may be at a loss, but if you ask how to chop firewood, isn't that a task he can accomplish with ease?"}
{"trans": "\"You are about to pay the price for your idleness.\" After speaking, the ox lowered its head back under the yoke and went on its way, pulling its cart full of fertilizer. You should live an upright and honorable life, and be mindful throughout your life never to be lazy or idle. If you still feel no shame, then you will surely suffer the bitter consequences."}
{"trans": "He painted it, and did so with such uncanny realism and lifelike vividness. His art deceived people's eyes time and again, causing them at first glance to take it for a real object, never imagining it was a painting. The art of painting, which for hundreds of years had been considered low-class, vulgar, and unfit for refined company, was only in his hands brought to prominence and glory once more."}
{"trans": "Throughout history, emperors have often been deified, considered sacred and inviolable, mysterious and unfathomable. Consequently, they were worshipped and regarded as deities, hence the title \"Son of Heaven.\" In reality, emperors were also human; some were good and some were bad. Among emperors, there were enlightened rulers of great talent and vision who brought peace and stability to the country, as well as fatuous and incompetent ones."}
{"trans": "There was a time when he 'threw his head back and laughed as he went out,' proclaiming, 'We are no men of the wild weeds.' The deceit and intrigue of officialdom, the sanctimony of the secular world, and the cruelty of reality cooled his burning passion. How could the golden banner of a 'court scholar' ever restrain your wild and untamable heart? At last, after the frustrations and suffering of an official career, you found peace."}
{"trans": "A suspicious-looking vagrant has been harassing our territory for quite some time now, stirring up trouble and sowing discord. I command you to apprehend this troublemaker by tomorrow and execute him. \"It will be done, my lord,\" the policemen said and left. The villain, being one-eyed, did not notice them winking at each other as they replied."}
{"trans": "Americans have never been ones to deify their national leaders, let alone obey their every word. But Obama succumbed to the expectations of his supporters. He too believed he was the long-awaited savior they had been hoping for. The silence of the people was a rude awakening for him. But the feeling of that meteoric rise was so intoxicating it almost made him lose himself."}
{"trans": "The Taurus man, usually amiable and smiling, is pragmatic to the core and absolutely cannot tolerate betrayal from within. Don't try to probe his true feelings. The steady, stable bull is content to graze, smiling at you like Jesus watching his flock. If you don't want the scene to shift to a Spanish bullring, witnessing a tragic spectacle of rivers of blood and bodies strewn everywhere, then whatever you do, don't make him jealous."}
{"trans": "For a Gemini, what's the fun in life without playing a few little tricks and showing off some cleverness? Don't be fooled by their usual carefree demeanor; once a situation becomes complex and intricate, they will strike when you least expect it. While everyone else fights tooth and nail, they can sit back and reap the benefits. Yet, just when they've succeeded in capturing everyone's attention, they will coolly walk away, playing a game of cat and mouse with you."}
{"trans": "But in reality, being in the Eurozone and bound by the euro, it is a complete fantasy for the Germans to think they can go it alone. The Eurozone was founded with the grand vision of shared prosperity; now that the Greek debt crisis has sparked the European debt crisis, shared loss is only to be expected. A fire at the city gate brings disaster to the fish in the moat—and what’s more, fires are already raging all around, with some areas nearly reduced to scorched earth."}
{"trans": "From this perspective, directly investing in commodities is perhaps the most direct, most effective way to hedge against inflation. However, when it comes to commodity investing, what often first comes to mind are shocking stories—of people going bankrupt speculating on commodity futures, of airlines suffering massive losses from crude oil hedging, and so on. It is as if the risks of commodity investing are so high that they deter the average person."}
{"trans": "The vast sky endures the fury of lightning and thunder, giving way to calm and sunny days; the boundless ocean endures the rage of stormy waves, giving way to tranquil, rippling waters; the silent forest endures the cruelty of the law of the jungle, giving way to the songs of birds and the fragrance of flowers. It is because the sky, the ocean, and the forest know how to endure, to yield at the right moment, and to humbly accept, that they possess their beauty and magnificence. Humility reveals a boundless world."}
{"trans": "Looking over the fast-changing landscape of the Gaokao in recent years, what we need is to firmly grasp the new trends and developments in exam design. By planning ahead, you can go from seeing things in a haze to having a crystal-clear understanding. Only then can you be as prepared as if you had a million elite soldiers at your command, ready for the fierce battle of the exam, where formidable foes are vanquished with ease. With guidance from master teachers, iron is turned into gold. A journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step; a nine-story tower is built from a mound of earth. Let us help you successfully take the first step in your Gaokao preparation"}
{"trans": "Learning to appreciate yourself is like having a golden key to happiness. Appreciating yourself is not about being narcissistic, nor is it about being self-aggrandizing or self-absorbed, and it is certainly not about being complacent. Give yourself some confidence, a little joy, and a smile, and how could you not find happiness in life? You should often celebrate yourself and learn to find a joyful state of mind."}
{"trans": "Perhaps people are gradually realizing that the internet and traditional industries are not mortal enemies in a zero-sum game, but rather a \"peerless duo\" that can form a perfect partnership. The retail industry is gradually showing signs of channel integration, with an increasing fusion of online and offline. Currently, Taobao has opened physical stores, while traditional enterprises are venturing into the internet in droves. From Gome and Suning Appliance to Walmart and Carrefour supermarkets, to name but a few."}
{"trans": "In summary, the commodity market is facing an endless stream of uncertainties, financial hegemony continues to manipulate the market, and the rules of the game are constantly changing. Against the backdrop of a global debt crisis and a world economic recovery with mixed prospects, the US dollar remains the most significant factor affecting commodity trends. With European and American economic policies exerting a constant influence and domestic controls adding to the volatility, the interplay between capital and confidence, interwoven with these three factors, will inevitably cause sharp fluctuations in the commodity market."}
{"trans": "China Red is a profound and intriguing spiritual culture, a spirit unique to the Chinese people. It is a powerful force, one of unremitting self-improvement, sincerity and earnestness, diligence and kindness, and an indomitable will. It is a Chinese quality, passed down for thousands of years and imbued with a vast and noble spirit.\n\nLian Tian Hong, a leading furniture company, carries the torch of China Red with immense passion. Guided by a business philosophy rooted in integrity, it pioneered transparency in its workshop production, setting a benchmark for the industry and giving the furniture sector a whole new look."}
{"trans": "Character Profile: In *Journey to the West*, Tang Sanzang is tall and well-built with a refined demeanor and a gentle temperament. His knowledge of Buddhist scripture is profound. On his westward journey to retrieve the sacred scriptures, he faces eighty-one trials, yet his resolve never wavers. With the help of Sun Wukong, Zhu Bajie, and Sha Wujing, he endures countless hardships and finally succeeds in bringing back the true scriptures. Sun Wukong, also known as Pilgrim, is Tang Sanzang's eldest disciple. He has a pair of Fiery Eyes with Golden Pupils, can perform the seventy-two transformations, and can ride the clouds and mist. He despises evil, fears no difficulty, and is persevering, heroic, and fearless."}
{"trans": "III. Approach to Studying: I have my own set of methods—\"cultivating a feel for the questions\" and \"avoiding late nights.\" In fact, once you become proficient through practice, while it may not be completely effortless, you can at least have a good grasp of the situation. I do not advocate for the \"sea of problems\" strategy, because we do not need to do the same or similar questions over and over again. We only need to compare and think deeply about similar problems to summarize the methods for solving that type of question. This \"feel for the questions\" is similar to what we often refer to as a \"feel for the language\" in English learning."}
{"trans": "The world's longest man-made structure – the Great Wall of China\n\nThe magnificent Great Wall of China traverses the peaks of lofty mountains across northern China. With a total length of over 6,700 kilometers, its construction began during the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods. It is a rare ancient military defense project in the history of human architecture. Renowned for its long history, vast scale, and majestic grandeur, it has been inscribed on the UNESCO \"World Heritage List\" and is hailed as the \"Eighth Wonder of the World.\""}
{"trans": "It is understated because it has the substance to be; it is understated because it is distinguished and cannot be common. In essence, luxury goods are exceptional, even unique. There is absolutely no room for compromise in their quality or their embodiment of fashion, and they possess an impeccable superiority in art, culture, and style. Luxury goods are by no means a necessity of life, but their unparalleled craftsmanship and profound brand heritage can certainly make them an essential part of it."}
{"trans": "Although her legs felt as if they were filled with lead, she still strode across the street toward the parking garage. Now, drowsy, she drove alone along the fluorescent-lit concrete lane, feeling her consciousness blur. When she had parked here seventeen hours ago, she had been flanked by two station wagons parked so close that she had to squeeze her way out of the car. Now, her beat-up Taurus sat like an orphan, solitary on the eighth level of the parking structure."}
{"trans": "Ten years ago, perhaps no one knew that the internet could move around with laptops, just as twenty years ago, people could not imagine that a telephone could fit in a pocket, constantly accompanying modern people in their work and life. And today, all sorts of smart devices are radiating an unprecedented magic, subtly changing the human way of life. In the next ten years, it is highly likely that more disruptive innovations will emerge in fields such as terminals, displays, virtual reality, information sharing, and consumer electronics"}
{"trans": "We advocate for team spirit, and we also admire the heroism of risking one's life to save a comrade. However, we must ensure that we do not become a burden or liability to our companions, and try our best not to let them take unnecessary risks to rescue us. Therefore, rigorous training and decisive judgment of the situation on-site are absolutely essential! Before you've been through a hail of bullets, don't play your armchair strategist games in front of us. Some things are not for a student officer fresh out of school like you to understand"}
{"trans": "It's my fault for not deeply grasping the five-character poem of the poplar tree and the seven-character poem of my grandfather. Although I've made some progress in the art of \"playing dumb\" over time, I have yet to achieve a great epiphany. Last night, my neck was bitten by a mute mosquito, and I shouted, \"How can you bite people without making a sound? How can you not suck blood in an open and honest way? Why can't you learn to be an upstanding mosquito and charge whistling toward my neck?\" Therefore, I live a life that is both exhausting and absurd, and I often hear the humming laughter of mosquitoes and insects."}
{"trans": "When barbecue joints spring up like mushrooms after the rain in every corner of the city, people's nightlife becomes increasingly flavorful. However, it is truly difficult to find a place that offers both delicious food and an elegant environment. As the setting sun casts a red glow upon the sky, meeting the lush green of the golf course, tables are artfully arranged according to the terrain. Amidst this beautiful scenery, the barbecue chef expertly grills the food, waiters weave through the crowd, and customers chat and laugh with contentment. Soothing music plays in the background, creating a truly charming atmosphere."}
{"trans": "Hearing me say this, the old gentleman let out a long sigh: \"Ah, I’ve had so many brushes with death, it’s truly a long story!\" And so, this proud, obstinate old gentleman, for the very first time, finally opened up his arduous life and inner world to me, a writer he had never met before... Through his telling—at times impassioned, at times somber, now with tears streaming down his old face, now breaking into hearty laughter—I sensed the magnificent yet tragic life of a septuagenarian who had endured the vicissitudes of life, as well as the inescapable hand of fate..."}
{"trans": "From this perspective, the Western political arena is far from being as clean as people imagine. Beneath the facade of integrity, there is a turbulent undercurrent of muck and mire, just that this muck and mire has a uniquely Western flavor. Onstage, they \"sow\" with high-sounding rhetoric; offstage, they \"reap\" in a reasonable and legal manner. The shrewd politicians and astute capitalists cooperate with a tacit understanding. The game of quid pro quo and power-for-money transactions between officials and businesses is played with such sophistication that it reaches a level of being open and legal, so sophisticated that even Western democracy and rule of law are helpless against it."}
{"trans": "After wading through water for several hours, a vast, wide-open cavern suddenly leaped into view. \"Heavens, this isn't an underground cave, it's a veritable subterranean forest,\" a team member exclaimed. The cavern was as large as a basketball court, its floor a rugged terrain of jumbled rocks, with water gurgling through crisscrossing ravines. Stalagmites as thick as bowls soared over 10 meters high, accompanied by massive stone curtains, jagged rock pendants, and intertwined stone pillars... a bizarre and motley collection resembling a primeval forest. Furthermore, countless crystalline formations glittered under the lights, making those walking among them feel as if they had entered a fairy-tale world."}
{"trans": "The heart's capacity can also shrink to be very, very small, holding only a home, a person, a sesame seed, a single virus. A thread of rain can drown it. A wisp of wind can shatter it. A single rumor can leave it in unbearable agony. A single plot can doom it beyond redemption. The heart can be hard, harder than any metal known to man. The heart can be soft, like a sorrowful plea, like the finest silk. The heart can be resilient; though damaged and wronged a thousand times, it remains as smooth as new. The heart can be brittle; with one careless slip, it shatters in an instant, its fragrance lost, its beauty broken like jade."}
{"trans": "Wandering through, one finds shady groves of trees and a profusion of flowers vying in beauty; the tranquil and serene scenery is a beauty beyond measure. A gentle breeze carries a delicate fragrance, leaving one feeling refreshed and uplifted. Winding stone-paved paths lead to secluded spots, flowers and trees are arranged with scattered elegance, and the crisp, pleasant sound of gurgling water fills the air. These multiple scenes are interwoven, forming a vibrant and fragrant tapestry of life.\n\nThe scenery changes with every step. Every flower and leaf, every blade of grass and tree, every rock and spring reveals its own unique charm, allowing one to escape the worldly clamor and experience the soul-cleansing beauty of being one with nature. To linger amidst such beautiful scenery is to fully savor the joys of life."}
{"trans": "The furious former whaling ship said, “This is the whale we caught.” The gloating current whaling ship said, “Then why is it here with us?” The furious side said, “It escaped again.” The gloating side said, “You should have tied it more securely.” The furious side said:"}
{"trans": "Your sanctimonious appearance cannot hide your arrogance and your greed! Phony doctors, babbling witches, and glib-tongued liars are mocking our incompetence—beware the slap! You, ungrateful husbands, think yourselves clever and capable. You despise love and kindness;"}
{"trans": "A two-month-long farce has died down. Why Foxconn was so enraged by these two reports, we do not know. A wealthy and powerful corporation does not lack think tanks, and besides tearful pleas, crisis PR tactics can also include high-profile anger: perhaps it was to make an example of one to warn a hundred, or perhaps it was a feint to distract."}
{"trans": "Reward according to merit. The delicious food should belong to it. \"I see you as pitiful and laughable, so arrogant and conceited,\" the wild duck replied. \"Your view is skewed. We wild ducks fly to the ends of the earth, carefree and without ties, making our home wherever we go. But you live under someone else's roof; we earn our own living, while you beg for food from others.\""}
{"trans": "The beginning of an article is the most difficult. It must be striking and forceful, get straight to the point, and be incisive in order to captivate the reader. There is no need to beat around the bush or use clichés. He also told me that when reasoning reaches a difficult and tangled point, introducing an analogy will readily resolve all the convoluted arguments. What economy, what skill! Insights of this kind"}
{"trans": "She began to divine with a serious air. After a flurry of activity, she pretended to examine the sand tray, made a few calculations, and then looked up at Kudi, saying, \"You damned, shameless scoundrel! How dare you lie to my face! The divination is perfectly clear. Your name is Kudi, and you are a murderous, plundering, utterly wicked, and unpardonable villain.\""}
{"trans": "The castle was truly a paradise. Gazing at the magnificent structure, he was utterly entranced, filled with amazement and envy. They strolled through the garden, picking fresh fruit to eat, and lost themselves in the delightful atmosphere of singing birds and fragrant flowers, lingering on with no desire to leave. By the time they had leisurely toured the entire castle, night had fallen. They returned to the great hall by the pool, planning to spend the night there."}
{"trans": "The air conditioning was indeed quite effective. The cool, fresh air, the orderly layout, the well-dressed guests, and the courteous waiters all made one feel that this place was a world of its own, exceptionally comfortable. It's always people who spoil the mood. Those well-dressed gentlemen were all greasy-faced from eating, which was quite unpleasant to look at. They all nodded to Nikolai, and for some reason, some even winked at him."}
{"trans": "The Japanese began to waver. Seeing that they were at the end of their rope and the tide had turned against them, they finally gave up resistance and fled toward the city. The armored trains continued to move back and forth, wiping out the remaining enemy forces. They were like two majestic, triumphant terrestrial dragons, advancing irresistibly and crushing everything in their path. They flattened the fortress fortifications where the enemy hid and sent the slower-running enemies to their graves. They were a soul-stirring victory declaration for the Chinese army, heralding the doomsday of the die-hard invaders."}
{"trans": "After this calamity, our family fell into a decline and never recovered. Our family fortune withered away, and we became impoverished and destitute, the whole family struggling in hunger and cold. The old locust tree, even more so, lost its former splendor. The remaining stump stood solitary and alone, forlorn in the semi-desolate courtyard, left to the mercy of the wind and rain, enduring the cold dew and frost. For many years it remained listless, neither sprouting buds nor growing branches, and completely ignored by everyone. It could only \"stand\" there rigidly, \"sighing up at the sky.\""}
{"trans": "Reactionary things will not fall if you don't hit them. Where the broom does not reach, the dust will not vanish of its own accord. You are all finished. Only I, Zhang Pujing, a loyal Bolshevik, am a man of one righteous spirit, two clean sleeves, three hearts and two minds, four feet to the sky, five limbs prostrated, with double-sixes for success, the July 7th Incident, the Eight Immortals crossing the sea, the Nine Provinces of the land, and ten-out-of-ten perfection. Haha, a revolution is not a dinner party, not writing an essay, not painting a picture or doing embroidery, not frying dough sticks. The proletariat has exercised its dictatorship over all of you demons and monsters."}
{"trans": "Excessive adulation of Gaokao top scorers can make them feel superior to others. Some may become complacent, arrogant, and self-important, and in their future work and life, unconsciously lower their standards for themselves, stagnate, and find it difficult to cooperate with others. Alternatively, others may believe they can never be inferior to anyone, leading them to excessively pursue perfection and become overly cautious, hesitant, and rigidly conventional in how they handle things. All of this can cause these former top scorers to lack creativity and passion in their endeavors, ultimately making it difficult for them to achieve great things."}
{"trans": "I will treat every one of my patients equally, regardless of whether their clothes are luxurious or shabby, whether their appearance is handsome or ugly, whether their finances are abundant or constrained, whether their status is noble or humble, whether they are spotless or covered in wounds, whether they have a difficult and complex disease or a minor ailment, whether they are courteous or rude to me, whether they are well-cultivated or illiterate, whether they are humorous and witty or dull and uninteresting, whether they are high-ranking officials or common folk..."}
{"trans": "Love is free. That involuntary admiration, that irrepressible yearning, that deep affection through thick and thin, that devoted love of mutual support in difficult times—these are life's deepest comfort and most solid reliance. And all of this is free. Moreover, it cannot be bought with money. Goals are free. Whether you are a prince living in luxury or a beggar in rags, as long as you are willing, you can set a goal for your life. This goal can be grand or ordinary, brilliant or simple; as long as you are willing, you can have it."}
{"trans": "Once you have set your goals, you should concentrate your energy, plan a path to success, and then forge ahead with all your might. During this time, you cannot be half-hearted, look around indecisively, or be easily distracted by new things. In this process, you should strictly eliminate doubt and fear, as they will sap your fighting spirit, ruin your forward momentum, and affect your actions toward achieving your goals with full effort. A person whose heart is filled with doubt and fear will ultimately accomplish nothing and have nothing. When the emotions of doubt and fear creep into your mind, your goals, energy, motivation, and all your strong thoughts will be severely impacted and crumble to pieces."}
{"trans": "A cultured person holds in reverence and admiration the many virtues of humanity, such as loyalty, courage, trust, diligence, mutual support, self-sacrifice, fearlessness in the face of danger, fortitude, and unyielding integrity. Not everyone may be able to embody these qualities, but they know to admire and celebrate them. A person can be cowardly or lazy, but they cannot disguise these vices as noble virtues. Because they are unable to be noble themselves, they cannot vilify all those who are as hypocrites. You can kneel in the mud, but you cannot smear that filth across the chest of the world and then gravely proclaim that everything is squalid."}
{"trans": "In this world, there are beginnings that are silent and beginnings that are spectacular; beginnings that are cautious and beginnings that are bold; beginnings that are secretive and beginnings that are reluctant; beginnings that harbor ulterior motives and beginnings that are open and honorable. For every stage of life and for every matter within it, different people will choose for themselves different ways to begin. These ways reflect a person's disposition, vision, courage, magnanimity, and breadth of mind in how they conduct their life."}
{"trans": "Generally speaking, the final stage of review plays a finishing-touch role in the entire preparation process. Therefore, we must pay great attention to this final review stage, while also avoiding mistakes that could lead to all previous efforts being wasted and causing us to miss out on success. The following explains several common pitfalls."}
{"trans": "Currently, the broader market is leaderless, with major sectors undergoing rotational adjustments and market support proving ineffective. Mainstream funds are finding it very difficult to trade, so the overall market is very weak. As skittish as a bird startled by the twang of a bow, it panics at any hint of bad news, which leads to intensified panic selling. Meanwhile, capital on the sidelines continues to wait and see. Coupled with the pre-holiday period, the appetite for going long is weak, causing market sentiment to become even more dissipated."}
{"trans": "The Japanese, putting up a desperate resistance, were already on their last legs. Their barely-held battle line, like a battered warship riddled with holes and leaking from all sides, immediately shattered like weathered rock upon receiving this fatal blow. In truth, stopping the armored train's advance was not difficult; one simply had to destroy the tracks ahead of time."}
{"trans": "Passing off Fakes as Genuine: How to Distinguish the Real from the Fake?\n\nCollectible walnuts are becoming known and loved by more and more people, and their market prices have risen accordingly. Consequently, counterfeits made with high-tech methods are also ubiquitous. Old Hu was also fooled when he first started out, but now, he is much more experienced and knowledgeable. He even clutches his walnuts while sleeping at night, and it's nearly impossible to fool him anymore. He shared a few tips with the readers of the Evening News on how to distinguish the genuine from the fake."}
{"trans": "In this life of mine, much like others, I have walked down broad avenues and crossed over single-plank bridges. Full of ups and downs, twists and turns, I have made my way through it all. I cannot but admit that I have been quite fortunate; the success I have achieved and the hollow reputation I have gained are all somewhat undeserved. On the other hand, I have also experienced misfortunes of a kind that few ordinary people ever could. During that appalling, so-called “Great Revolution,” I nearly lost my life for daring to speak out for justice. The physical suffering, too, is something I will never forget."}
{"trans": "Zhuge Liang can be called one of the greatest military strategists in Chinese history. In Longzhong, he compared himself to Guan Zhong—what self-confidence that was! And how accurate was his prediction of the tripartite division of the realm! When he first emerged, he faced Xiahou Dun's army of one hundred thousand without the slightest fear; by setting fire to Bowangpo, he won the sincere admiration of Guan Yu and Zhang Fei; by setting fire to Xinye, he terrified Cao Ren; with his \"silk pouch stratagems,\" he infuriated Zhou Yu three times, demonstrating a cleverness not without a touch of humor; utterly loyal, he did not proclaim himself emperor despite receiving special permission from Liu Bei; forced to implement the \"empty fort strategy\" due to a decision-making error, his calmness is truly admirable. However, what moves me most is not any of these, but rather, \"autumn winds on the Wuzhang Plains.\""}
{"trans": "When night fell in the mountain village, we would hear bursts of sharp, rising and falling dog barks. After midnight, the barking would become faint and intermittent. Lights in the windows lit up one after another, like cabbage hearts with their pale, clean faces... The moonlight was also washing its face by the water pipe in the courtyard..."}
{"trans": "A shot from point-blank range, skied; a close-range header, inexplicably wide; even one-on-one chances were not converted... Lacking regular playing time in the domestic league, the National Youth Team's ability to convert chances up front is lamentable, and this is currently the team's greatest weakness."}
{"trans": "But in a sociological sense, today's Palestinians and Israelis are sworn enemies. The reasons lie both in the strangeness, indifference, suspicion, and hostility that arose after the Jewish people's great exodus from Palestine in 556 BC scattered them across the world, separating the two \"brothers\" for over a thousand years, unable to meet; and in the return of the Jewish people two thousand years later to Palestine to establish the state of Israel. As a result, their mutual struggles turned into brutal slaughter, and any sense of brotherhood vanished without a trace."}
{"trans": "National policies descend from on high, and the industry situation takes a sharp downturn. Ceramics production areas are bearing the brunt, struggling like trapped beasts. The production regions are struggling; ceramics enterprises are searching for a path forward. While this cold wave brings impact and suffering to the production areas, it also allows them to see the road ahead with greater clarity.\n\nRead more: With the industry in a sharp downturn, ceramics production areas struggle like trapped beasts."}
{"trans": "Stepping into a Suzhou garden and wandering through its courtyards, one might see small bridges over flowing water and white-washed walls with dark-grey tiles; or winding paths leading to tranquil seclusion and new vistas appearing at every turn; or a landscape that changes with every step, in endless variation. The most profound impression is that its rigorous and sophisticated garden-crafting process is of the same masterful quality as that of the Imperial Garden in the Forbidden City. The arrangement of corridors, pavilions, and waterside terraces, advancing in staggered yet harmonious layers, makes a 20-meter path give the impression of a wide, open view stretching for several miles, immediately evoking the feeling of strolling through a painting."}
{"trans": "The situation on the battlefield is by nature fluid and unpredictable, and since ancient times, there has been no fixed formula for achieving victory. How to deploy troops and generals; whether to attack or defend; to engage in positional or mobile warfare; to voluntarily abandon a position or hold a city; which sectors to give up and which must be defended—these are all matters that should be decided by the frontline commanders themselves, without excessive interference from the high command. In truth, any strategy or tactic can be integrated and applied flexibly, and it is difficult to claim that any one method of warfare is inherently superior. This is a case of \"all roads lead to Rome\"; winning the battle is the only hard truth."}
{"trans": "Fiction is the backlight of the soul; you pour a part of your soul into the work, giving it your own flesh and blood, and thus it achieves artistic height. This involves two issues. First, a writer needs to examine the true state of their own soul; you must first shape yourself. Second, the power of sincerity is immense. Here, the significance of sincerity is not just about overcorrecting, but also about abandoning a creative climate of affectation, fawning, grandstanding, and opportunism. Do not scratch an itch from outside your boots, do not make a simple thing needlessly convoluted, and do not peddle sincerity like a quack's cure-all plaster. I believe sincerity should be an attitude toward life, especially for a writer."}
{"trans": "The weather-beaten locust tree by the window,\nthe moon, returning to its place, hangs motionless on the motionless locust branches, like a bell, yet so far from the sound of a bell.\nI know, one cannot expect dust to ring like gold when it hits the ground.\nAnd yet, it is precisely this dust-like moonlight that makes the silent midnight more like a sunken midnight ship."}
{"trans": "Cruel and ruthless, cold and unfeeling, treacherous and sinister, yet I am kind-hearted and merciful, charitable and benevolent, delivering all living beings from suffering. In the gloomy Mansion of Dis, my spirit was drawing talismans and characters with intense concentration, when suddenly came the pained voice of Dulcinea del Toboso, and I learned that she had unfortunately fallen under a spell,"}
{"trans": "Suspected of tearing down the bridge after crossing the river\n\nInternet analyst Xu Dandan believes that Alibaba is suspected of tearing down the bridge after crossing the river. “Capital has its own nature, and it is only right and proper for them to consider the interests of their own shareholders. Accepting an investment means accepting a capital contract. You cannot worship it as a god when you need it, and denounce it as a demon when it gets in your way.”"}
{"trans": "Even the most discerning gaze was helpless! Eyes were dazzled, daring not to look directly at the throne. We came from the East, but the incident occurred in the West; the ranks were both wide and long—stretching from end to end beyond sight. As some fell, others stepped forward, scrambling to be first, their courage overflowing. With long spears in hand, one could fight ten, and ten could fight a hundred. Countless lives vanished without a trace."}
{"trans": "Just when he had snatched the emerald and was beside himself with glee, he had to leave on a long trip for business. When he returned home, he learned from his family that the emerald, along with a bag of gold and silver, had vanished without a trace. Upon hearing this news, he flew into a rage, fuming and glaring, hitting and cursing himself, and even tearing his own clothes. The more he thought about how all his efforts had come to nothing, the angrier he became."}
{"trans": "2008, an extraordinary year. And in the domestic arena of corporate public relations and marketing, it was a veritable bloodbath. For corporate marketing and PR staff, it seemed as if \"PR crises\" had descended overnight like a deluge or a savage beast, striking terror into their hearts. Time flies, and in the blink of an eye, we are about to enter 2009. Looking back on this year, perhaps many business owners are still shaken, treading as if on thin ice."}
{"trans": "At work, Leo friends will take on almost anything. As long as they can showcase their talents and abilities, they will grit their teeth and help out. By doing so, however, they can easily become forgetful, and their accumulated workload can be considerable. Therefore, a handy calendar and a weekly planner are the most suitable good-luck items for the office. A lively, whimsical style is especially fitting, as it perfectly matches their pure, child-like nature, enabling them to tackle problems with ease no matter how busy they get."}
{"trans": "An excellent essay should contain six or seven types of fine sentences: first, vivid and beautiful metaphors; second, magnificent and powerful parallelisms; third, emotionally rich personifications; fourth, profound allusions and adaptations; fifth, thought-provoking philosophical sentences; sixth, mutually complementary contrasting sentences; and seventh, agile and wonderful poetic sentences. Skillful use of these fine sentences makes the language concise and the meaning profound, adds the finishing touch, and illuminates the entire piece, allowing one sentence to do the work of ten and naturally enhancing the literary grace of the writing."}
{"trans": "What's even more ridiculous is that Western countries' economies are still in a slump, their money-printing machines are itching to start up again, and their treasuries are running on empty. Yet, they once again expect China to \"feed\" the world... Everyone is now turning their gaze to China, the world's second-largest economy. If the post-Cold War global system controlled by the United States is on its last legs, will this emerging economic superpower step up? Or will it let the West suffer the consequences of its own actions? But this time, China may be willing but unable to help."}
{"trans": "Emperor Wu of Han, Liu Che, reigned for fifty-four years, during which he expanded the frontiers and territories, forming a vast domain for China that extended from Liaodong and central Korea in the east, to the shores of modern-day Lake Balkhash in the west, reached the heartland of Vietnam in the south, and stretched to the line of the Sayan Mountains in Outer Mongolia in the north. The population under his rule reached nearly sixty million. To be fair, among the hundreds of emperors in Chinese history, those who could expand frontiers and territories like Emperor Wu of Han, Liu Che, were few and far between. For the others, it was already considered an achievement just to hold on to their inheritance and not become prodigal sons who surrendered sovereignty and brought humiliation to the country. In this sense alone, Emperor Wu of Han, Liu Che, deserves to be called an emperor of great talent and bold vision."}
{"trans": "His short stories often reflect the trivial matters of ordinary people's lives, lacking plots with dramatic twists and turns. Yet they feel refreshingly new because of their distinctly original perspectives and an unquestionable personal leaning. He excels at creating unique and individualized characters, which is precisely the hallmark of a genius writer."}
{"trans": "Some people bluff and bluster, using borrowed authority to build themselves up, readily seeking selling points by claiming to be \"number one in the industry\" or \"the first of its kind.\" They package themselves and, amidst a deluge of information, incessantly promote and market themselves. Then there are others who, embracing the mindset of not flaunting their wealth, or of simply focusing on their own affairs, conceal their capabilities and bide their time, accumulating strength for a later release. They simply put their heads down and work in silence."}
{"trans": "In fact, why are these \"wars of words\" always so overwhelming, lasting for months on end without stopping? What forces are fanning the flames from behind the scenes? And what is it that is subtly influencing the judgment and thoughts of netizens? It's not just ordinary netizens; even some website administrators are deeply perplexed by this."}
{"trans": "In the evening, he spread out his manuscript paper, preparing to write an article to earn back that 20 yuan. That night, the title of his article was: On the Necessity and Possibility of Morality? He wrote: \"In this age of degenerate materialism, we must hold high the banner of morality to oppose enslavement by material things.\" That night, his essay advanced with triumphant force, fierce with grief and indignation, deeply distressed yet patiently guiding, boiling with passion and heart-rending, utterly sincere, sharp and unrestrained, fluid and soaring, pouring forth like a river for a thousand miles…"}
{"trans": "1. Shenlun essays must be far-sighted. As a form of official-style writing, their most fundamental characteristic is a lofty conception and an elevated theme. Shenlun writing must focus on long-term interests, look beyond the surface to see the essence, be adept at taking a commanding, strategic view, and be skilled at elevating the theme. In other words, the resulting articles must possess a noble ideological plane; they must absolutely not pander to vulgar tastes, let alone be base and vulgar or filled with complaints and grievances."}
{"trans": "He is often melancholic, but his melancholy is compassion for the suffering. When he smiles, his smile is like the yearning of those who seek the unknown, like stardust falling on a child's eyelids, and like a piece of bread in the throat."}
{"trans": "And if you look further up, how beautifully proportioned her shoulders are! Like twin lambs, and like two jade peaks; as slender as autumn hills, and as level as autumn waters. Above the shoulders, one comes to the face, the focus of common odes and praise."}
{"trans": "Even the lotus suffers the tragedy of having its nine familial clans exterminated. Lotuses link to one another, the thousand fingers of their petals grasping a summer, then letting a summer go. Now, it is the ghostly rain of an autumn night, pattering down on the surface of shattered duckweed, like a disheveled, blind-eyed Chopin tormenting a piano of a thousand keys."}
{"trans": "Listening to Zen under the moon, its meaning grows ever more profound; discussing swords under the moon, one's spirit grows ever more true; discussing poetry under the moon, its grace grows ever more subtle; gazing upon a beauty under the moon, affection grows ever more sincere.\nThe way to enjoy the moon: when it is bright and clear, gaze upwards; when it is hazy, gaze downwards.\nSpring wind is like wine; summer wind is like tea; autumn wind is like smoke; winter wind is like ginger and mustard."}
{"trans": "He rode the waves of blood, aboard a small red boat, arriving gracefully in this damp and cold world. His final leap was to be shot from the gate of life by a rolling wave of heat, like a torrent of mud and sand in a bursting flood. The infant's frenzied cries were like glass cups colliding and shattering."}
{"trans": "If China has one more gloomy writer, this north-facing study should be held responsible. Sitting in the shadow of this north-facing window, I am like a fruit pregnant with the south, chilled in a refrigerator. By day, I seem to be submerged at the bottom of the sea, where the silent gloom plays its gray music."}
{"trans": "The red candle has a soul; it does not begrudge its life, yet it clings to life with exceptional fondness. Whenever it is about to burn out, the scorched wick, like a spine, rises high and convulsively in the flame, holding firm, strikingly resembling a valiant warrior suddenly struck by a bullet, frozen in mid-air as a defiant sculpture, before collapsing heavily into a pool of fiery red blood..."}
{"trans": "Go out and gaze at the sky; the heavens have their warp and weft, by which to examine one's own conduct. After the morning dew and rain, the sun emerges. Silver pearls adorn everything, all shining in unison, as one sun gives birth to countless suns. An old web in the corner need not be swept away. Let the dust settle on it; over time, its strands will thicken like the gnarled roots of an old tree, serving as a three-dimensional mural. In it, one can read tradition and modernity, finding something new with every reading."}
{"trans": "Spring always knocks first on the garden's bamboo gate, and then, one by one, lights the torches extinguished last year. At this time, winter, alone in its empty house, is like a wailing wolf, abandoning its post and fleeing from the sudden flames. The flames leave its lonely gaze bewildered, and the earth's floral skirt makes it lose its way."}
{"trans": "The means of punishing evil are more evil than evil itself; the wanton slaughter of the innocent is as effortless as cutting down weeds. Beneath the banners of loyalty and righteousness lies a world of flashing swords; the price of grandeur is a land strewn with corpses. The fundamental value of life is that of a few withered blades of grass under a horse's hoof; the people's right to exist is that of a few bubbles at the edge of a vortex."}
{"trans": "—Celestial music, the mystery of the infinite, the siege of the infinite against the finite, or God's stern yet gentle command. Music is the omniscient and omnipotent God of musical notes; He is both the Creator—who arranges and defines the positions of the notes—and the Savior, if the notes can but hearken to and follow that unceasing, unremitting sounding."}
{"trans": "Above the winding lotus pond, the eye is met with a field of overlapping leaves. The leaves rise high out of the water, like the skirts of graceful dancing girls. Dotted among the layers of leaves are white blossoms, some blooming gracefully, others budding shyly; they are like lustrous pearls, like stars in an azure sky, like beauties fresh from the bath."}
{"trans": "Therefore, as for a city, if everything one encounters and sees is young and bright, it must be that it has not yet reached maturity, its strings not yet in tune. This is like writing: when adjectives are like a young woman in heavy makeup and parallel sentences are like lovers side-by-side, one has certainly not yet mastered the art of prose. The epitome of writing is like an old street with its sparse plane trees, the old benches beneath them, the idle chatter between the seats—exquisite and rambling."}
{"trans": "The suffering of one family does not affect the happiness of the world. Summer arrived irrepressibly, the silk tree flowers like pink powder puffs, caressing the lonely and desolate windowsill of the hospital room. The girl's head had become a polygon, its long-healed sutures, like cracked earth, were splitting open into black holes beneath the thin skin, an inner demon swelling in all directions."}
{"trans": "Sitting in the shadow of this north-facing window, I am like a fruit pregnant with the South, kept cold in a refrigerator. By day, I seem to be immersed at the bottom of the sea, the silent darkness playing gray music. At night, I seem to hear the sound of Eskimo sleds gliding, and the long beard of the North Star hangs down, striking with a clang against strings of white stalactites."}
{"trans": "They are as quiet as the bright moon, not wanting to disturb anyone, yet they in fact startle a wide area all around. This can truly be called \"the rising moon startling the mountain birds.\" Compared to the quietness of Tao Yuanming, Wang Wei's has more of an aristocratic air and a more exquisite design. His genius lies in an aristocracy that is more plebeian than the common folk, and a design that is more natural than nature itself."}
{"trans": "Even the brown coffee table is painted with feeling, a gentle little rectangle bearing all the liveliness upon it. There is another, larger piece, also with white magnolias, thin and luminous, like mist and crystal, like the coolness of the jade fish that Yang Guifei held in her mouth for a toothache. The winter jasmine’s strong, tenacious lines expand and contract with force; its control over life is at once serene and tyrannical."}
{"trans": "\"Misty and boundless, I shall return to the Great Wilderness!\" Reading this passage, I didn't feel sadness, but rather a sense of beauty. It was a feeling akin to reading the *Records of the Grand Historian* and coming to the part where Jing Ke, dressed in white, crosses the Yi River to assassinate the King of Qin—it's full of color. Just imagine: a young master from a wealthy family, having seen through the ways of the world, stands barefoot in the snow with a shaved head and a red cloak to bid farewell to his father. How beautiful that is"}
{"trans": "We cannot bypass that dark, humiliating, and indifferent era, just as upon approaching the Mogao Caves, we are forced to confront that ugly and desolate Daoist's Pagoda. No matter how splendid, colorful, and magnificent the Dunhuang of memory may be, it can never fill the vast emptiness of the Library Cave, wash away the ghastly pallor within the Thousand Buddha Caves, or heal the bleeding wounds of the ancient nation on the horizon."}
{"trans": "Forgetting visits us with increasing frequency and becomes a friend loyal unto death. I make use of this friend as a filter; what is forgotten was never necessary to remember. The subconscious manipulates memory, just as the wind directs a kite on an unseen plane. New, formidable warp and weft are woven into the uneven mesh of memory's sieve, and the remaining curds are like cheese, their sweetness wrapped around hard lumps that grate against the teeth."}
{"trans": "His chest was broad and straight, holding the earth-shattering roars of his ancestors as they chased ferocious beasts. His pelvis curved in sharply, the sharp, prominent bones indicating where powerful muscle groups had once attached, like the ropes of the Yellow River boat trackers, pulling the entire torso in gecko-like climbs. And his limbs, like the long, beautiful tusks of an African elephant, gleamed with the smooth luster of polished jade."}
{"trans": "It was not until years later that I could dispel my lingering resentment and admit that that day had been a treacherous turn in my life. What compelled me to destroy that painting of withered roses was not the art teacher's scorn, but the sight of your talent, so brilliant and dazzling that I broke my paintbrush myself, with a gesture of bitter regret. What is regret like? It is like a small mole on the body—only you know its position and the story of how it came to be."}
{"trans": "On the balcony, I was combing my hair with a fine-toothed comb, and my hair fell out like autumn leaves, strand after strand, brushing against my arms like a night rain. In the distance and nearby, many car horns blared in a flurry. The gradually darkening sky unfolded on all sides like a vast lake of mist and rosy clouds. From a window on the lowest floor of a building opposite, a wisp of pale cooking smoke emerged, rising with great hesitation, as if unsure of where the sky was."}
{"trans": "With a kettle in each of her two hands, she reminded me of a woman walking with spirited poise on a slender tightrope. This was all due to her waist; her waist, her waist was so supple that, in Bai Shui's words, it made me feel as if I were eating Suzhou nougat. It wasn't just her waist. My diary puts it well: she had a set of curves that rivaled the rosy clouds in beauty and vied with the moon on the water for ethereal grace, weaving a vast, bewitching web"}
{"trans": "Because of your language, you fall silent, and sinking to the very depths, you let yourself disappear.\" Losing language, and thus losing the self, losing the entity of existence, Yang Lian therefore realized he had become a \"ghost.\" A ghost, drifting between cities, observing with a cold eye (like an insect, a ghost is also an \"it,\" able only to observe), observing the complete annihilation of that thing called \"meaning.\" There is a sea, but the harbor means nothing to you."}
{"trans": "The streetlights are far from one another; between stretches of brightness are stretches of darkness. My shadow at times appears in the light, and at others vanishes into the darkness. A wind from out of nowhere stirs the colorful fallen leaves wave after wave, as if stirring the impressions of life. I feel as if I am this intangible wind, able to perceive its own existence only when it strips and swirls the colorful fallen leaves."}
{"trans": "But we who stood on the open land—my father, my elder brother, my relatives, and many fellow villagers who had shed blood and sweat, been scorched by the sun, and drenched by the wind and rain—stood there stunned, listening to the distant thunder, with no one showing any sign of going inside to take shelter from the northwest rain. Our hearts were heavier than the parched sky. Everyone was silent, for our hearts too were skies about to rain, and this rain of the heart seemed even more tragic, more vast and unceasing, than the northwest rain."}
{"trans": "Imagine, if Tang Bohu and the Qianlong Emperor were to be considered \"first-rate ancient Chinese poets,\" then we could only quietly burn the entire *Complete Tang Poems*.\n\nThe same is true of calligraphy. When an astonishingly high price is paid for a piece of mediocre brushwork, it is as if a pile of gold has crushed the scales of Chinese calligraphy's history.\n\nFaced with this situation, I once sighed deeply: \"When the cultural lineage recedes, small hills are hailed as peaks; when the mighty-winged have flown far, tattered feathers pass for the great Peng.\""}
{"trans": "The seizure came on abruptly, as if the girl had received a divine command from on high, and she suddenly convulsed without any warning. The soft-bodied girl shriveled up like an extremely hard rolling pin, every muscle gleaming like iron. Her small body, like a bow bent for shooting eagles, arched back on the snow-white hospital bed. Countless beads of sweat dripped from this grotesque human rainbow bridge, like melting snow from the eaves when spring warms and flowers bloom."}
{"trans": "This unleashed power is solitary and immense, at once following and breaking conventions, a fusion of drunken intoxication and poetic sentiment, akin to the Dionysian spirit described by Nietzsche. Channeling this Dionysian spirit, Zhang Xu wielded his brush as if in a drunken stagger. Swaying and gesticulating, with an elegant bearing and boundless energy, he completed all his movements in one fluid motion, then tossed his cup aside with a laugh and fell into a deep sleep. Little did Zhang Xu know that this drunken gait of his was also the very stride of Tang Dynasty culture."}
{"trans": "But just as I was holding my breath, listening with rapt attention, they suddenly, as if by common consent, all fell silent. Those cicadas, they startled me again! The cicada song was like a rope that had bound my heart tightly; then suddenly, without any warning, it was loosened, and my heart, completely unprepared, came undone—like a wave that strives to leap for the sky, only to tumble back onto the sand! When had summer stepped over the threshold to arrive? I hadn't even noticed"}
{"trans": "I once shed silent tears for your humiliation and despair. My tears were like a stream, clear as crystal, yet they could not wash away your thick layers of filth, but only rinsed the scales from my own eyes. These tears could not in the slightest dampen your hearts of iron and stone, but only extinguished the anxious, fervent flames in my own heart. Now, at your suffering, I laugh—a laugh that roars like thunder. It does not come after the storm, but resounds before it."}
{"trans": "I gradually came to understand that in the face of disaster, one cannot use the grammar of disaster; there is another grammar that leads directly to a poetic realm of spiritual freedom. From this point on, my state of being was no longer the same. Each time I read chapters from Zhuangzi such as \"Autumn Floods,\" \"Free and Easy Wandering,\" \"Discussion on Making All Things Equal,\" and \"Under Heaven,\" it was like looking at my own electrocardiograms. For such an intimate ancient philosopher, I find it difficult to make a calm and impartial assessment, and so I can only shy away."}
{"trans": "You rest your forehead against the gold-woven embroidery. When the sun was on this side, it scorched the golden threads, but now they are cold. People of the past lived out their strenuous lives, and all they did and all they were gradually became covered in dust; their descendants, when airing out clothes, would shake that dust off again, and it would dance in the yellow sun. If memory had a scent, it would be the fragrance of camphor—sweet and reassuring, like a clearly remembered happiness; sweet and melancholy, like a forgotten sorrow."}
{"trans": "It is this very quality that brought them close to life, and that allows us, a thousand years later, to still feel close to them.\n\nYang Guifei's vibrancy, I imagine, was like a ceramic hot water bottle, warm and gentle as jade, placed at the foot of the bed. As the water inside gradually cools, it leaves one with a feeling of gentle stubbornness.\n\nSu Qing, however, was a small red clay stove. It had its own independent fire, with visible crimson flames and the crackling, popping sounds of its burning. But it was rather difficult to tend to—adding coal and wood, the smoke would choke you."}
{"trans": "Like surging tides, like roaring waves, it beats against the emotions settled in the depths of your heart. In an instant, you feel the cicada's song, like a furious tide washing the sand, has snatched away the faint sorrow you clutched so tightly in your hand. The urgent song of the cicada, at its highest crescendo, suddenly comes to an abrupt halt. It is more like a magnificent essay being violently torn apart, its sonorous words and phrases scattering to the ground, striking the earth with the clang of metal and stone. Then, in silent desolation, they become but tattered fragments, leaving behind only a sense of melancholy and sadness. What is this, if not the song of life?"}
{"trans": "Here there is vibrant youth, and fiery, passionate hearts. There are no decadent little old men, only robust old youths. The silvery moon wandered slow-footed in the vast, empty sky; she seemed to be weeping, hating her own solitude.... And there was the swimming pool, always like a benevolent old woman, smiling with a face full of wrinkles. When she saw many lively children leap into the water like little mermaids, splash after splash, she was so happy that she burst with huge, silvery sprays of water."}
{"trans": "For thousands of years, it has been a wasteland of lost traditions. On one side of the wasteland are the faint, unknown great artisans; on the other is a bewildered contemporary woman. The kilns on both sides blaze fiercely, like two beacon towers separated by thousands of mountains and rivers, yet the signals they transmit are precise and unmistakable. Countless other beacon towers are related to war; only these two are not. Separated by the bloodshed of the Three Kingdoms, the struggles of the Sui and Tang, and the carnage of the Song, Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties, there are only two wisps of the purest, gentle smoke, calling to each other across the distance."}
{"trans": "Lin Daiyu's brokenness lies in her unforgettable, heart-wrenching love; Sanmao's brokenness stems from a moment of lucid clarity and transcendence after weathering the vicissitudes of life; Van Gogh's brokenness is the sun using a golden knife to make him suffer excruciating pain in the midst of light; Beethoven's brokenness is the tragic and heroic opus of life, struck from the collision of supremely spiritual black and white keys. If the brokenness of ordinary people reveals the purest and most beautiful glimmers of humanity, then the brokenness of these extraordinary souls is like silver fireworks blossoming across the sky above us."}
{"trans": "You can wash your face several times a day, yet it's still not clean; you never wash your eyes in your entire life, yet they are always bright.\n\nEmpty spaces can't be reached by mops or brooms, so there's no point in spending money on them; besides, people are mobile, natural dust-repelling pearls.\n\nStrangely, the air doesn't grow thin just because the space is small. To see the breath of a person, one can use a cigarette as a visible form of air: inhale one puff, exhale three, and watch it curl and spiral to the ceiling. As the auspicious clouds gather, one can first survey the abundance of things below, and then gaze up at the vastness of the universe above."}
{"trans": "To know oneself without first knowing others is merely a way of huddling in a corner, living and dying in isolation. It is to be no more than a summer insect with whom one cannot speak of ice, a frog at the bottom of a well, or a donkey in a mill.\n\nOnly by being able to know others can one truly have self-awareness; it is just like Princess Iron Fan's fan—you must be able to expand it before you can retract it.\n\nThe more you know, the wider your engagement with the world. By dispersing yourself throughout the world, seeping into all things and matters to observe their sizes and shapes, their weights and densities, only then can you, by dissecting with the finest precision, gradually come to recognize your own true self."}
{"trans": "The song is like drifting clouds and flowing water, allowing one to cast off worries and wander serenely within it. It is also like surging billows and terrifying waves, beating against the emotions settled in the depths of your heart. In an instant, you feel the cicada's song, like a fierce wave washing over sand, has snatched away the light sorrow you clutched so tightly in your hand. The urgent cry of the cicada, coming to an abrupt halt at its highest crescendo, is like a magnificent piece of writing being violently torn apart, scattering sonorous words and phrases that fall to the ground with the clang of metal and stone. Afterwards, in the silence and desolation, what remains becomes but fragmented scrolls and broken pages, leaving one with only a touch of melancholy and a hint of wistful sadness."}
{"trans": "How can there be nothingness? The affairs of gods are unknown to man; the affairs of man are to construct and carry on meaning amidst the siege of fate—helplessly, one might say—thereby saving both the present and eternity. Therefore, \"Eternal Recurrence\" is by no means \"a total affirmation of earthly life,\" but merely says that man cannot escape earthly life, not even through death. As for affirmation, it points toward an eternal pursuit and transcendence, that is, the earth gazing up at the firmament—that vast and profound music which has always filled the heavens and the earth."}
{"trans": "Looking at Master Hsing Yun's Zen paintings, however, is a completely different experience. The feeling is akin to reading the pastoral poems of Tao Yuanming and Wang Wei in the dead of night. A few short brushstrokes, a light touch of ink—they do not stir up surging emotions in the soul, but rather, they quiet our inner turmoil. It is not that something is being stuffed into our hearts; instead, it is as if the worldly cares that originally filled them are being cleansed away. It is like a mountain stream after a downpour: the water is at first turbid, but once the storm passes and the sky clears, the stream becomes completely limpid, and the mud and filth in the mountains are washed clean away."}
{"trans": "The sound was by turns slow and rapid, high and low, loud and deep, intermittent and continuous. At times, it was like the chime of gold and the resonance of jade; at times, like the solemn tones of great bells and pipes; at times, like large and small pearls dropping onto a jade plate; at times, like red and white coral sinking into the sea; at times, like the strumming of an unadorned zither; at times, like a dancing thunderbolt; at times, like a hundred birds vying in song; at times, like a rabbit darting down and a falcon soaring up. My imagination ran wild and I could not contain myself. My heart burst with joy, and my writing flowed with inspired ease. The dead characters seemed to come to life, and I, too, felt as if I were once again brimming with youthful vitality. I have rarely in my life reached such a spiritual state, one that is all the more difficult to convey to others."}
{"trans": "Perhaps clouds are meant to journey across the sky; unlike the sun with its feet like fiery wheels, they won't descend to earth to accept my warm welcome. It is merely my illusion, but an illusion that is all too beautiful.\n\nIf the blue sky is a wordless celestial book, the clouds must be its wordless footnotes, and the tracks of my speeding car, translating the language of the clouds onto the road, are the most recently published commentary.\n\nThe sky sets the scene with its shifting blues, the clouds sketch with clean strokes, and then hand it to my eyes for printing. We are all narrating a tale of Kuafu."}
{"trans": "It was inconvenient to take it out and eat on the plane; when I got back to America and tried it, it was greasy and too spicy, nothing like the sausage roll I would occasionally get from my father.\n\nIn Shanghai, our home was right next to Kiessling's Café, newly moved from Tianjin during the war. Every day at dawn, they would bake bread, sounding an olfactory alarm. A magnificent, fragrant energy would break through the air with the momentum of a wind spanning thousands of miles, and yet it was the gentlest of alarm clocks. The problem was, it went off at the wrong time, waking one up for nothing, leaving one feeling as helpless as one does before the vexing beauty of spring.\n\nHaving this \"fragrant\" neighbor was, in reality, a disturbance."}
{"trans": "In life, happiness is like the sugar cube used to entice a child to take medicine, and even more like the electric rabbit at a dog track that lures the dogs to race. A few minutes or a few days of happiness makes us live a whole life, enduring much suffering. We hope for it to come, hope for it to stay, and hope for it to come again—these three phrases summarize the entire history of human endeavor. While we pursue and wait, life slips away unnoticed. Perhaps we are merely chips for time to consume, and to have lived a lifetime is but to serve as a sacrificial offering for those very years, without ever giving happiness a thought."}
{"trans": "There was a dark, olive-green silk, across which swept large black shadows, pregnant with wind and thunder. There was also a silk Japanese fabric, pale lake-blue, shimmering with wood-grain and water-ripple patterns; at intervals, two teacup-sized plum blossoms floated on the water, rendered with sharp, calligraphic strokes, like a stained-glass window in a medieval chapel, the red glass set in a heavy iron frame. Most common on the market were all sorts of unnamable colors—not quite green, not quite grey, not quite yellow, colors fit only for a background. These were all neutral colors, also called protective colors, also called civilized colors, also called mixed colors."}
{"trans": "This all comes from her movements; her every step, every stretch of her waist, every brush of her hair, every glance, every dip of her head, down to the slight flutter of her sleeves and the gentle dance of her skirt, are all like flowing honey, like a gentle ripple in the wind. How could we not rejoice and marvel?\n\nThe most lovely of all is that soft waist of hers. The ancients spoke of a weeping willow in the wind, and \"Dream of the Red Chamber\" speaks of Qingwen's \"water-snake waist\"; both are used to describe a slender, soft waist. But the waist that I adore is just like Suzhou's chewy nougat, filling my tongue with sweetness and my teeth with softness.\n\nWith a waist so soft, her limbs naturally possess an ethereal and extraordinary grace."}
{"trans": "To the side, some wine bottles leaned crookedly, with hardly any wine left. It's a pity I am not a drinker; otherwise, at a time like this, taking two or three sips would surely be the finest enjoyment. \"Raise a cup to invite the bright moon,\" and the moon would shyly slip into your cup. A gentle breeze swept by, and you could hear the woods on the mountains on either side rustling. Amidst the swaying of the branches, the lights flickered in and out of sight, just like a mischievous child playing hide-and-seek with you. In concert with the leaping, scale-like glimmers on the river's surface, it was as if they were performing an enchanting concerto together. What kind of earthly dreamscape is this?"}
{"trans": "I can no longer explain how, with a bit of real sun shining on the stage, there could be such a sense of desolation. A space between art and reality overlapped, becoming hazy and indistinct; it was like holding a lit match in the sunlight—faint and wavering, its pale orange flame was barely visible, yet you could feel all the more clearly that your own hand, in that sunlight, was also something transient… The maidservant on stage sang for a while, then, holding a tea tray, she moved with the grace of parting flowers and brushing aside willows, passing through rooms and crossing countless thresholds to arrive in the study. There she bowed deeply to the Young Master Cousin, raising the tea tray to the level of her brow."}
{"trans": "I saw a tabby cat lying languidly at the foot of a dilapidated, abandoned wall, where a profusion of morning glories bloomed in a riot of deep blues and vivid purples. The cat stretched indifferently.\n\nIn the hazy night, I saw a streetlight casting the shadow of a transformer box from the sidewalk haphazardly onto a white construction wall. Intertwined with the dancing shadows of the roadside trees, it looked just like the balcony where Romeo sang his love song to Juliet.\n\nI saw the face of the poet Chou Meng-tieh. As I waved goodbye to him, it was perfectly framed in the small window of a moving bus, as if the entire vehicle was, with great ceremony, serving as his picture frame."}
{"trans": "Imagine that pure, unadorned heart of a child, which desires and yet loves everything, watching as, in the hazy morning mist, no matter how you beckon with your hands, it simply will not return to your breast. What a void this is! And so, Mr. Pingbo, feeling terribly stifled, honestly and earnestly, like a gentle spring breeze whispering among the green trees, softly and intimately, confides in you about his memorable yet ungraspable \"childhood.\" Although he cannot dwell permanently in that \"childhood,\" if he can call a few more companions to linger there with him a few times, it can also slightly lessen his feeling of emptiness, and that taste of melancholy will no longer constantly cloy at the back of his tongue."}
{"trans": "You could also say it is the starting point of the Chinese people's journey to modernity, but what other nation's steps toward modernity have been as hurried, flustered, diffident, and chaotic as those in Shanghai? You could also say it is an urban civilization that rose in opposition to agricultural civilization, but what other urban civilization, like Shanghai, has always been so deeply coveted, dismantled, besieged, and shrouded by the immeasurably vast forces of the countryside? In short, it is a great paradox: when you gaze upon its sordidness, it erupts in a dazzling brilliance; when you worship its mighty power, it turns around to show you its scar-ridden back wall."}
{"trans": "However, the reality was just the opposite. I sat there quietly, listening to the sound of raindrops overhead. At this moment, sound was better than silence. I felt a boundless joy in my heart, as if I had drunk immortal dew and tasted the finest cream, feeling utterly ecstatic, as if I could float away and become an immortal. This sound was at times slow and at times rapid, at times high-pitched and at times low, at times loud and at times deep, at times intermittent and at times continuous. At times, it was like the chime of gold and the resonance of jade; at times, like the grand tones of ancient bells; at times, like large and small pearls dropping onto a jade plate; at times, like red and white coral sinking into the sea; at times, like the plucking of a simple zither; at times, like a dancing thunderbolt; at times, like a hundred birds vying in song; at times, like the drop of a rabbit and the rise of a falcon. My imagination ran wild and I could not contain myself; my heart burst into bloom, and words flowed from my pen as if carried by the wind."}
{"trans": "All around was a jumble of dazzling billboards and shops, car horns honking. He was like the man from the ancient legend of the Yellow Millet Dream, but he had only woken from a sleep and had not had such a dream—which engendered an even greater pathos. … The Taoist priest walked to the front of a hardware store and prostrated himself. Of course, they had no money for him, and he, as if no one else was there, vacantly performed a kowtow and that was that. Scrambling to his feet, tapping 'tok—tok—', he moved to the entrance of the cigarette and paper shop next door and once again 'knelt down in the dust and dirt,' his head hanging askew, his movements a black, stagnant current, like a black chrysanthemum slowly blooming."}
{"trans": "So what of it? Birds chirp in the spring, crickets in the autumn, and mosquitoes buzz like thunder in the summer. At night, insects awaken while birds sleep. Wind and rain are not a daily occurrence. A dog will not bark if no one approaches; a hen will not cackle if it has not laid an egg. Only man, with his language, his actions, and his machines, makes sound at all times and in all places. Even when alone in a room, with no one for conversation, he can turn on a phonograph, listen to a radio, and even in sleep release a thunderous snore. Language, of course, is not merely sound. But when speech is unpleasant, unwanted, or heard indistinctly through walls and across a distance, the words themselves lose their sharp edges and clear outlines, becoming a fluctuating din, as lacking in meaning as the crowing of roosters and the barking of dogs."}
{"trans": "In the passage of time, what is past is gone forever, unable to be rewound to the moment when faces and peach blossoms first met. Yet, in human memory, the lingering charm or sorrow of the past often surges back like waves against the shore, making the present an extension of that past charm or sorrow, which continues to grow as one moves forward.\n\nIf life were a blank book, pre-bound and paginated, the disturbances of past events would be like writing on a page with too much force, the ink seeping through to the subsequent pages, impossible to erase. Of course, there is no need to destroy the old pages to feel gratified, just as there is no need to destroy the Yellow Crane Tower simply because the yellow crane has already departed. However, it is necessary to spread open the old history under the lamplight, to proceed where one ought to proceed, and to stop where one ought to stop."}
{"trans": "The Great Northwest is a land of barren basalt mountains and vast deserts where a lone column of smoke rises straight into the air. You, a geologist, have journeyed for twenty years with a large canvas geology bag slung over your shoulder.\n\nFor twenty years, your tent in the sea of sand dunes has been like a small boat under the curve of a cold moon. Could the sound of the bell from Hanshan Temple outside Suzhou city really reach your \"guest boat at midnight\"? Your wife, a beauty as fair as a flower, gazes into the distance for you, her face a net of longing, trying to retrieve a love's heart that has drifted far away.\n\nYou return home once a year, and each time, you plant a clump of sacred bamboo in front of the door. But then you leave again, leaving her with nothing but clumps of bamboo leaves shaped like the character for \"one\" or \"individual.\""}
{"trans": "If life is a blank book, bound and paginated in advance, the way past events disturb us is like pressing too hard with the pen when writing on one page, so that the ink seeps through to the next few, leaving a mark that cannot be erased. Of course, there is no need to destroy the old pages to feel gratified, just as since the Yellow Crane has already departed, why destroy the Yellow Crane Tower? However, it is necessary to spread open the annals of the past under the lamplight, to proceed where one ought to proceed, and to stop where one ought to stop. After fully embracing life, one can look upon old events or new things with tolerance and generosity, giving them the position and meaning they deserve. If it is a beautiful thing, one can see what kind of branches will sprout anew from its bud; if it is a sorrowful thing, one also sees a tender shoot that starts on a cloudy day and reaches into the sunshine."}
{"trans": "Everything felt distant from me; even my own shadow in the bright moonlight looked hazier than smoke and mist. I was just about to turn and go to sleep; for some reason, my feet hesitated for a step, and the arrow-swift remnant of a dream paused abruptly, though it immediately sped off again like an arrow loosed from its bow."}
{"trans": "In the end, all one hopes for is a form of aesthetic support.\nLike a sinking ship in an icy sea, utterly beyond rescue, looking up to see the tableau of dark, churning clouds;\nlike facing certain death under a flurry of blades, looking down to see the pattern of spattered blood.\nAesthetic support is the final support."}
{"trans": "What we glean from the past and the future are but seeds. When you were a wish wandering in the mists, I wandered there as well. We searched for each other, and from our longing grew dreams; those dreams were unending, those dreams were boundless."}
{"trans": "In such purity, the dish is oneself, the cook is oneself, and the one eating is still oneself.\nJust as love is oneself, the one who knows this love is oneself, and the one who recalls it is still oneself.\nOne savors oneself sip by sip; through the cup of time, one intoxicates oneself."}
{"trans": "The trousers clung tightly, making it impossible to take a step. My heart was as pure as a sheet of paper, as blank as a newborn's.\nI bid farewell to the sea, and to the lonely seabird circling in the sky. I will return, to speak of the dust from the ends of the earth.\nPicking up my umbrella and shoes, picking up the myriad things of the human world."}
{"trans": "It is unpredictable. I only hope that, even as a human flaw, it should present itself with some dignity. Like the furrowed brow and sigh of a noble warrior, not the midnight brawl of a mob of ragged soldiers; like the millennial standoff of two snow-capped peaks, not a tangle of messy vines strangling a tree trunk."}
{"trans": "That night on the plateau was exceptionally beautiful. The sky was like a blue sacrificial vessel fired during the Jingtai reign of the Ming dynasty, profound and boundless. Through the moon, as enormous as a silver table, one could still see the metallic blue light behind it. The moonlight, unable to overpower the blue hue of the sky, drifted like diluted ink, in wisps and threads."}
{"trans": "Watching the same movie over and over is like spitting out the food you've eaten and chewing it again. In that moment, the tops of our heads turned golden, as if covered by the beards of wheat. I remembered a character in a book by Gorky who once said that lovers from one's youth should not meet again, for it is like a skeleton rising from the ground..."}
{"trans": "With my eyes closed, I think of it; with them open, I long for it. And yet when all of this came rushing towards me, threatening to overwhelm me, I found I simply no longer knew what I truly wanted. All I felt was a relentless ache in my heart, as if it were being squeezed tight by threads of silk, seared by dripping candle tears, and tossed about on a vast, boundless sea."}
{"trans": "The echo of your faintest sobs becomes life itself. When its face is veiled, it is covered and hidden, just like your greatest souls. When life speaks, all the winds become words; when life speaks again, the smiles on your lips and the tears in your eyes will also become words."}
{"trans": "As a result, the collective cultural character grew increasingly dim. As spring passed and autumn came, as plum blossoms withered and cranes aged, culture became a purposeless waste, and a closed-off pursuit of moral perfection led to an overall immorality. The advance of civilization was thus cancelled, leaving behind a pile of plum petals and crane feathers, tucked like bookmarks in the annals of the national spirit."}
{"trans": "To judge the religious spirit by the standards of science and philosophy is like trying to fall in love using intellect and reason; it will inevitably suppress the passion of life and exhaust the charm of love. To replace the religious spirit with political and economic policies is like viewing family status and wealth as the conditions for marriage, sacrificing one's children to become reproductive machines and spiritual eunuchs."}
{"trans": "In truth, all destruction is tangible, all grievances are difficult to articulate, and therefore, those answers are also questionable. What is not to be doubted are the results—the withered grass and broken tiles that bear witness to one disaster after another, from ancient times to the present. I am willing to face all of this in a perilous situation without any protective mechanism, like taking off a glove to touch an old man's scar."}
{"trans": "Now, I have written all these pages. I see myself as a child copying a verse from a long ode, one the angels sang when God fashioned the first man. Or perhaps I am like an illiterate, memorizing a sentence from a book written by wisdom, inscribed on the title page of emotion before time began."}
{"trans": "Even grand events that span a millennium, even magnificent feats that connect ten thousand miles, if they belong to culture, are often borne on a single pair of aged shoulders. Like smugglers, like fugitives, they dodge and make covert inquiries along the way, searching for a place of entrustment. The decision of entrustment is always written in a will, and thus it is fated that they cannot explain the cultural meaning of their own lives while still living."}
{"trans": "The result is beyond doubt: withered grass and broken tiles, bearing the weight of one disaster after another from antiquity to the present. I am willing to face all of this in a perilous situation, utterly without protection, just as one might take off a glove to touch the scars of an old person. This touch often leads to a painful reflection: as the very foundation of our existence, Chinese civilization is also riddled with scars, yet how has it avoided a total collapse?"}
{"trans": "Can anyone really slip a ready-made meaning into your birth certificate and hand it to you? The very act of being born is a demand for meaning from the one who is born, requiring you to seek or create it. It is like a publishing contract with an advance on royalties, where the term is a lifetime. Of course, you are not the creditor; you are the debtor. It is life that demands meaning from you, so it's not your place to complain about anyone."}
{"trans": "Those uneven inks, those red-inked headlines, those crossed-out names, those rows of exclamation marks, those cautious appeals, those repeated alterations, combined into a terrifying sound that rushed upon you. This is like how merely a few strands of white hair on a spiderweb in a corner, or half a scrap of cloth among the withered leaves on a treetop, can instantly bring us back to that era we dare not think of again."}
{"trans": "Some land on the hair buns of the women picking vegetables, some stick to the swallows' black coats, and some slide into the small river to play in the water. The river, like a thousand wandering flute players, accompanies the joyful singing of children after school. When a mischievous child balances their books atop a yellow hat and wades across the river, shoes in hand, will the spore-babies pop out from the flute holes, bite onto the hem of the child's clothes, and finally return to the soil of their wild fern mother?"}
{"trans": "Oh, soul, come back! Return to your old home. When he spoke the Minnan dialect and made people roar with laughter, when he spoke the Beijing dialect and made people stare at each other in bewilderment, why did he never defend himself: that here, his Chu accent was as profound as the heavens, earth, mountains, and rivers, as magnificent as the blue sky, ghosts, and gods? Every syllable from the master of ceremonies, like his father's voice reciting the \"Chen Qing Biao,\" was mournful and sorrowful, each one striking you a heavy blow."}
{"trans": "He was too thin; his withered skin could not fill the gaps between his ribs, and the flat disc of his stomach was like a steel bridge built upon them. His heartbeat was violent and heavy, like a bleeding sun burning hollowly, emitting the sounds of flames and caves. Mei Ying had heard Yue Beizhi's heartbeat. It was deep and sonorous; through his well-developed musculature, that heart was like coal buried deep in the Earth's crust, pulsating with a steady and profound rhythm."}
{"trans": "From one inn to another, for one who has never settled in a mountain village or by the waterside, what is the talisman I can pray for? Perhaps it is youth, but how fleeting it is. I am like a person who has dug up a treasure, carrying pearls, gold, and silver in a leaky net bag, only to find it growing lighter and lighter along the way, as youth has already scattered into dust. Perhaps it is the scriptures and classics, but how can great learning mend the broken mirror in my hands?"}
{"trans": "In the mountain passes of his hometown, people would greet each other from a great distance. It was never the soft \"Hey—\" of city dwellers, nor the feigned \"Wow—\" of southerners. \"Hey\" and \"wow\" wouldn't travel far before being swallowed up by the mountains. Only the deep, resonant, long, and desolate \"Eha—\" would soar like a goshawk, lingering for a long, long time. Now, this hawk-like cry seemed to fall from the sky like a mist, and an endless homesickness, both hot and poignant, filled Shen Zhanping's heart."}
{"trans": "Hurry and escape from the political quagmire that even oneself could not fully comprehend, to pursue the long-lost poetic sentiment. To chase after poetic sentiment is also to chase after the self—the self once known throughout the land, the self his wife clung to, the self he thought he had found, but had instead lost. This time, as he turns back to give chase, he is sent off by the morning glow, with the river's current as his witness, the cries of apes as his encouragement, and ten thousand mountains making way. And so, the vessel of life, once burdened ever more heavily, has become a light skiff once again."}
{"trans": "A plane may be thought of as Zhuangzi's free and easy wandering or Liezi's journey riding the wind, but it is not much to look at. It is nothing like a long train journey, with its hypnotic rhythm and ever-changing scenery. Looking out the window, you feel at once in the human world and yet departing for a world beyond. That is why when I travel abroad, wherever the clanging twin rails can reach, I always stand on the platform—a \"long pavilion\" in the truest sense—and wait for that train of masculine beauty to come thundering in, kicking its way into the station with unstoppable momentum, to carry me to faraway places."}
{"trans": "What stands in opposition to prominence is the ordinary; what stands in opposition to officialdom are the common people. The ordinary life of common folk who have disappeared into some small town offers greater concealment than the wild grasses and dense trees of the mountains and forests.\n\nConcealment in the mountains and forests retains and flaunts a proud solitude, and such aloof concealment is ultimately insincere. Concealment amidst the streets of a small town, however, not only does not require one to deliberately torment and destroy one's life, but on the contrary, allows one to live in great comfort, letting life settle snugly into a corner that is both tranquil and convenient, almost to the point of dissolving oneself from the outside in. It therefore becomes the highest form of concealment.\n\nPerhaps to speak of concealment is too narrow. In my mind, at any rate, the images of \"a small bridge, flowing water, and a home,\" and the \"longing for water shield and perch,\" are all ecological symbols of a spiritual philosophy of life."}
{"trans": "Spring breeze, we are not acquainted; why do you enter my silk curtains?"}
{"trans": "Henceforth I have no heart for the lovely night;\nLet the bright moon go down behind the western tower."}
{"trans": "The candle has a heart and is also loath to part, shedding tears on our behalf until dawn."}
{"trans": "Chang'e must regret stealing the elixir,\nFacing the vast sea and azure sky, a lonely heart night after night."}
{"trans": "Thrice sought to chart a course for the whole realm,\nTwo reigns he served with a loyal statesman's heart."}
{"trans": "His winding stratagems divided the empire in three;\nFor all eternity, a single feather in the clouds."}
{"trans": "A strain of 'Backyard Flowers', a plaintive grief unbearable to hear."}
{"trans": "The swallows that once graced the halls of Wang and Xie now fly into the homes of ordinary people."}
{"trans": "Melancholy, with no way to see Fan Li;\nEast of the Five Lakes, the misty trees are uneven."}
{"trans": "Had the east wind not granted Zhou Yu its favor,\nThe two Qiaos would have been locked deep in the Bronze Sparrow Terrace in spring."}
{"trans": "Victory and defeat are unpredictable in a soldier's life;\nA true man is one who can endure shame and humiliation."}
{"trans": "How can forty-eight years as emperor compare to the Lu family having a Mo Chou?"}
{"trans": "The general's commission departs the Phoenix Palace; the iron cavalry encircles Dragon City."}
{"trans": "The Qiang flute need not blame the willows, for the spring wind does not cross Jade Gate Pass."}
{"trans": "Our golden armor is worn through by a hundred battles in the yellow sand;\nWe will not return until we have conquered Loulan."}
{"trans": "The moon of the Qin Dynasty, the passes of the Han;\nThe army marched ten thousand li, but none have yet returned."}
{"trans": "The soldier at the frontier gazes upon the border landscape;\nlonging for home, his face is full of sorrow."}
{"trans": "To shoot a man, first shoot his horse. To capture thieves, first capture their king."}
{"trans": "The boundless desert is a lattice of ice a hundred fathoms deep,\nThe sorrowful clouds are bleakly frozen for ten thousand miles."}
{"trans": "The woods are dark and the grass is startled by the wind; the general draws his bow in the night."}
{"trans": "The desert sand is like snow,\nThe Yanshan moon is like a hook."}
{"trans": "Hoeing grain shoots under the midday sun,\nSweat drips down to the soil beneath."}
{"trans": "A horseman raises a cloud of red dust, and the royal consort smiles; no one knows it is lychees that have arrived."}
{"trans": "My dappled horse and thousand-gold fur,\nCall the boy to take them out for fine wine,\nAnd together we will dissolve the sorrows of all time."}
{"trans": "I want to cross the Yellow River, but ice blocks the stream;\nI will climb the Taihang Mountains, but snow fills the mountains."}
{"trans": "Summoned by the Emperor, he will not board the boat,\nHe calls himself an Immortal of Wine."}
{"trans": "Nature has concentrated its divine beauty;\nThe shady and sunny slopes divide dusk from dawn."}
{"trans": "My soul is lost and cannot be recalled; the rooster crows, and the world turns white."}
{"trans": "Thinking of the endlessness of heaven and earth,\nalone, I am overcome by sorrow, and my tears stream down."}
{"trans": "The boundless falling leaves rustle down; the endless river comes rolling in."}
{"trans": "Riding high on the spring breeze, my horse's hooves are swift.\nIn a single day, I see all the flowers of Chang'an."}
{"trans": "Zhuangzi's dawn dream is lost in a butterfly,\nEmperor Wang's spring heart is entrusted to the cuckoo."}
{"trans": "A lone plume of smoke rises straight from the vast desert;\nOver the long river, the setting sun is round."}
{"trans": "Sitting alone in the secluded bamboo grove,\nI play the zither and whistle long."}
{"trans": "The torrent plunges straight down three thousand feet, as if the Milky Way were falling from the Ninth Heaven."}
{"trans": "The window frames the Western Hills' snow of a thousand autumns; the door moors boats from faraway Eastern Wu."}
{"trans": "A riot of blossoms begins to dazzle the eye;\nThe tender grass just covers the horse's hooves."}
{"trans": "Four hundred and eighty temples of the Southern Dynasties, how many towers and pavilions are veiled in misty rain."}
{"trans": "A drizzling rain falls on the Qingming Festival;\nTravelers on the road are heartbroken."}
{"trans": "I wonder who tailored these slender leaves;\nThe spring breeze of the second month is like a pair of scissors."}
{"trans": "In the world of men, April's flowers have all faded;\nAt the mountain temple, the peach blossoms are just beginning to bloom."}
{"trans": "A bosom friend afar brings a distant land near."}
{"trans": "If friends and relatives in Luoyang ask about me, tell them my heart is a piece of ice in a jade pot."}
{"trans": "I urge you to drink one more cup of wine;\nWest of Yangguan Pass, there will be no old friends."}
{"trans": "I entrust my sorrowful heart to the bright moon, to follow you all the way to Yelang in the west."}
{"trans": "Though the water of Peach Blossom Pool is a thousand feet deep,\nIt is not as deep as the friendship of Wang Lun seeing me off."}
{"trans": "The lone sail's distant shadow vanishes into the azure sky,\nLeaving only the Yangtze River flowing to the horizon."}
{"trans": "The temple deep in the green bamboo grove,\nThe distant toll of the evening bell."}
{"trans": "The closer I get to home, the more apprehensive I feel, and dare not ask for news from the people I meet."}
{"trans": "In the melody this night, I hear ‘Breaking the Willow.’ Who would not be stirred by feelings for their homeland?"}
{"trans": "The beacon fires of war have burned for three months; a letter from home is worth ten thousand pieces of gold."}
{"trans": "How can the heart of a blade of grass repay the warmth of the spring sun?"}
{"trans": "A faint flute sound drifts from among the reed blossoms; a line of white birds is suddenly startled into flight."}
{"trans": "The tide-rider stands at the crest of the waves, holding a red flag that does not get wet."}
{"trans": "The grass, lush and endless, grows by roads running north, south, east, and west."}
{"trans": "The morning chill is light beyond the hazy green willows;\nThe spirit of spring is a riot on the red apricot boughs."}
{"trans": "I stop early on the fragrant grassy path, my journey but half complete;\nA late spring day, still chill and poised to rain."}
{"trans": "Late at night, the wind so gentle the fragrance was slow to come; who would have known that by dawn, the south-facing branches were all in bloom."}
{"trans": "What need is there for light green or deep red?\nIt is in its own right the first among all flowers."}
{"trans": "If trees had feelings, they would not be so green."}
{"trans": "The moonlight and dew are cold; the yellow leaves of the phoenix tree are drifting."}
{"trans": "I often remember our parting, on Jingshu Tower, the bright moon like water."}
{"trans": "When the night is late and the wind is still, at the time of my return,\nthere is only a river of bright moonlight, like a sheet of blue-green glass."}
{"trans": "My dream of my homeland's mountains and rivers is broken, separated by layer upon layer of misty water."}
{"trans": "The silk sash has not yet been tied into a lover's knot,\nAnd the tide at the river's edge has already receded."}
{"trans": "On and on, a thousand miles of misty waves; the evening haze sinks heavy and deep across the vast southern sky."}
{"trans": "With tear-filled eyes I ask the flowers, but they do not speak;\na flurry of fallen petals drifts past the swing."}
{"trans": "Amidst falling flowers, a person stands alone; in the light rain, a pair of swallows flies."}
{"trans": "A lonely grave a thousand miles away, nowhere to speak of this desolation."}
{"trans": "Day after day I think of you, but I cannot see you;\nWe both drink the water of the Yangtze River."}
{"trans": "Though the vows remain, a letter is hard to send."}
{"trans": "The old affairs of the Six Dynasties have followed the flowing water, but a cold mist and withered grass congeal into green."}
{"trans": "With a feather fan and a silk scarf, between talk and laughter, the enemy's masts and oars were turned to flying ash and smoke."}
{"trans": "Hearing the cries of several cranes in the night, everyone said the king's army was about to arrive."}
{"trans": "If one is to have a son, he should be like Sun Zhongmou."}
{"trans": "Thirty years of fame and merit are but dust and earth,\nAn eight-thousand-league journey with the clouds and the moon."}
{"trans": "Beneath the Foli Shrine, a scene of sacred crows and festival drums."}
{"trans": "Returning drunk, I stroke my sword and lean against the west wind; the river waves are still mighty, as is the will of man."}
{"trans": "The river surges like a whale, the mountains crouch like a tiger, a natural stronghold not of human design."}
{"trans": "The mist is lost in the watchtowers, the moon obscured at the ferry crossing."}
{"trans": "The Qiang flute plays in the clear day, caltrop-picking songs drift on the night; happy and playful, the old fisherman and the lotus maidens."}
{"trans": "The fragrance of rice blossoms tells of a bountiful year, amid a chorus of croaking frogs."}
{"trans": "A hundred fleeting years of rise and fall and farewells,\nYet you and I still face the same moon of that time."}
{"trans": "Helplessly, the flowers fall; seemingly familiar, the swallows return."}
{"trans": "At the mountain's foot, short orchid shoots dip into the stream,\nThe sandy path through the pines is clean and free of mud,\nA cuckoo cries in the pattering evening rain."}
{"trans": "The wutong tree, and a fine drizzle besides, dripping drop by drop until dusk."}
{"trans": "Swallows fly in, asking where spring is; only the pond is green of itself."}
{"trans": "I long to ride the wind and return, yet I fear the crystal towers and jade palaces, so high, would be unbearably cold."}
{"trans": "It was all too clearly a blissful dream;\nI turn to the east wind, my robe drenched with tears."}
{"trans": "The vast southern sky, a thousand miles of clear autumn; the water flows to meet the sky, an autumn without bounds."}
{"trans": "Red beans grow in the southern land;\nWhen spring comes, how many branches sprout?"}
{"trans": "The sun shines in the east, while it rains in the west.\nYou say it's a day without sun, yet the sun shines."}
{"trans": "Where Xie Ting bid farewell, the scenery always evokes sorrow."}
{"trans": "His deeds overshadowed the Three Kingdoms; his fame was forged by the Eightfold Battle Array."}
{"trans": "Gather flowers when they are in bloom; do not wait until they are gone to break a bare branch."}
{"trans": "The setting sun is infinitely beautiful, it's just that it is near dusk."}
{"trans": "Pity the third night of the ninth month,\nThe dew is like pearls, the moon like a bow."}
{"trans": "After gathering from a hundred flowers to make honey, for whom is the toil, for whom is the sweetness?"}
{"trans": "A thousand miles of yellow clouds dim the sun;\nThe north wind blows the wild geese in the swirling snow."}
{"trans": "A sudden rain from the front mountain comes across the stream, washing away all the summer heat of the world."}
{"trans": "The water is a sidelong glance; the mountains are knitted brows."}
{"trans": "The sparse rain passes, the wind makes the forest dance and break, a canopy of mist and banners of cloud."}
{"trans": "A passerby outside the wall, a fair lady's laughter inside."}
{"trans": "My sorrow for you is like the moon over the river tower; briefly full, only to wane again."}
{"trans": "I will draw the carved bow like a full moon,\ngaze to the northwest,\nand shoot the Heavenly Wolf."}
{"trans": "The surging river flows on, finding no rest; the fleeting light sinks swiftly in the west."}
